WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.680 --> 00:01:11.840
Parts 1 and 2

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:42.670
Preamble
One 

00:01:42.670 --> 00:02:54.639
item 

00:02:54.639 --> 00:03:00.790
of housekeeping, and one more significant
item - the housekeeping item is to say that

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:06.719
there is no fire alarm planned over the next
couple of hours, and I say that with a smile

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:12.589
because there was an unplanned one yesterday
which resulted in the evacuation of the building,

00:03:12.589 --> 00:03:18.700
so the systems are not entirely 100% operational.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.689
And if there is an alarm, by the way, members
of our staff will just escort everybody off

00:03:22.689 --> 00:03:23.689
the premises.

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:28.430
NHS England
The second is to just dwell on the nature

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:32.510
of the body for which we have responsibility.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:40.170
NHS England assumed its full range of responsibilities
from the 1st April.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:46.169
Up until the 1st April we had been operating
as a body with a limited range of responsibilities,

00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:51.159
the most important of which was the authorising
of the clinical commissioning groups.

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:57.700
211 of them have now been fully authorised
and are in operation.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:04.709
Rather more substantially from us, in terms
of establishment, has been the following array

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:07.389
of staff transfers.

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:12.729
At the outset of the government's proposals
for reform there was something like 50,000

00:04:12.729 --> 00:04:18.759
people working on commissioning across the
NHS in England -- that number has dropped

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:26.569
by about 17,500 as a consequence of natural
attrition and job loss.

00:04:26.569 --> 00:04:33.350
Today the numbers working in commissioning
directly are probably about 50% of the previous

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.030
resource.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:42.310
Up until 1st April the numbers on our payroll
were probably under 100, albeit other staff

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:48.270
were seconded in, many staff were working
for us on a half-time basis.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:54.770
The organisational effort that has gone into
establishing NHS England has been done by

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:59.759
a relatively small group of exceptionally
dedicated and hardworking people, and on behalf

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:05.330
of the whole Board I would today like to pay
tribute to the exceptional work that has gone

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.330
on.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.819
Jo Wass will refer to some more of that this
morning in her HR report.

00:05:10.819 --> 00:05:19.830
To reflect on where we are today is to observe
those numbers have risen to over 7,000.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:26.729
From the 1st April our establishment figure
was around 7,600 -- of those close to about

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:35.990
7,000-odd are filled, and another 2,200 have
arrived with us by a bulk transfer operation.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:43.450
This means that people have come from something
like 162 different organisations to join NHS

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.450
England.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:49.680
Some of the organisations from which they
have come have been seeing staff come, not

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.180
only to NHS England, but to maybe as many
as 32 other organisations.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:00.820
It has been like a multidimensional game of
chess in which movements have been through

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:06.930
systems and through time, in a manner which
has tested the organisational skills of all

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.550
the organisations.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:16.200
So 162 other organisations no longer exist;
the NHS has been significantly changed and

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.080
significantly reformed.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.360
I say this to stress that this has not been
a smooth linear process; this has been an

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.300
immensely complex process and I think it is
a great tribute to those who have joined us

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.939
that there has been such a continuity of support
as almost to disguise the complexity of the

00:06:36.939 --> 00:06:40.120
transfer arrangements.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:47.120
We look forward, now that we have our staff
and now that we are working as an almost fully

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.199
staffed up organisation, to starting to undertake
the transformation of care for patients in

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.490
the NHS that we know is absolutely necessary.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:01.659
Declarations of Interest
I turn now to the formal business of the meeting

00:07:01.659 --> 00:07:04.849
and invite any declarations of interest in
matters on the agenda.

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:07.039
I have not had any prior notifications.

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:08.039
No?

00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:09.039
Thank you.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.919
Minutes of previous meeting
So let us then turn to the minutes of the

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:12.349
previous meeting.

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:17.430
May I assume that the minutes are a fair and
accurate summary?

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.300
Thank you very much.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:24.560
I turn then to matters arising and I will
invite Bill McCarthy to talk to us about some

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:25.849
corporate governance items.

00:07:25.849 --> 00:07:26.849
Bill?

00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:28.729
Matters arising
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman.

00:07:28.729 --> 00:07:30.690
There are two matters arising.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:39.139
The first is a set of draft terms of reference
for a number of the committees that the Board

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:42.460
agreed to set up when we last met in April.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:51.710
They are draft and I expect there is still
some room for simplifying and sharpening them,

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:55.760
but I would welcome any comments from members
of the Board.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:02.009
The second matter arising is an anomaly that
we picked up very recently in the standing

00:08:02.009 --> 00:08:08.819
orders where the use of the seal would have
required a resolution of the whole Board.

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:14.699
That would be very unusual and not very practical
so I am looking for the Board's agreement

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:22.300
that we would amend the standing orders to
two executive members or one executive and

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.430
a Chairman or the Board's secretary could
authorise use of the seal, and all use of

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:32.020
the seal would be reported to the Board at
least twice a year -- so those two corporate

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.020
governance items.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.500
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.270
I will seek the approval of the Board on the
draft terms of reference to allowing them

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:45.450
to be signed off by me and the Chief Executive,
but if I could work with one or two of the

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:50.820
other non-executives on a programme of simplifying
and clarifying those terms of reference, I

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.570
would be grateful for that.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:56.690
Are we happy to approve that, and are we happy
to approve the proposal regarding the use

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.430
of the seal?

00:08:58.430 --> 00:08:59.430
We are?

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:00.430
Thank you very much.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:00.439
We turn now to the Chief Executive's report,
David?

